{
  "id": "COMPOUND:12208 - PROTEIN:1787",
  "verbalization": "The visible evidence suggests a potential mechanism by which the Influenza B virus hemagglutinin antigen interacts with the Immunoglobulin lambda variable 3-21. This interaction is mediated through the drug Selgantolimod, which acts as a target of the copper chaperone for superoxide dismutase, triosephosphate isomerase, and serum amyloid P-component. These targets are all involved in cellular processes that may be disrupted by the hemagglutinin antigen, leading to a possible functional or structural interaction with the immunoglobulin. The shared pathway of targeting by Selgantolimod implies a common mechanism of action, where the hemagglutinin antigen may influence the cellular environment in a way that affects the function of the immunoglobulin. The evidence supports a mechanistic link between the hemagglutinin and the immunoglobulin, though the exact nature of the interaction remains to be elucidated.",
